{
  "example": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_path_method`\n\nFor the `ReviewNodeData` enum above, a requirement with the dotted name `a.b.c` is\nrepresented by `ReviewNodeData::Requirement(vec![\"a\".into(), \"b\".into(), \"c\".into()])`.\nIts `node_path` implementation maps that identity to\n`[\"requirement\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example is concrete. It gives the identity `ReviewNodeData::Requirement(vec![\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])` and expects `node_path` to return `[\"requirement\",\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]`.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]` builds the same case with its local enum. The enum is named `ReviewNodeId` instead of `ReviewNodeData` and has the same variants. The test builds `ReviewNodeId::Requirement(vec![\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])`, interns it, and calls `Domain::node_path`. It asserts `path.segments() == [\"requirement\",\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]`, which checks the example's stated outcome directly.\n\nThe cited `requirements.sdk.graph.node_path_method` also says equal node data gives the same path across caches. The test checks this two ways. It interns the same data in a fresh domain's cache and calls `node_path` on the original domain. It also calls `node_path` on the fresh domain. Both paths are asserted equal to the first.\n\nThe test's `ReviewDomain` `node_path` (`impl[ReviewDomain/Domain]/fn[node_path]`) prepends \"requirement\" to the parts, so the mapping the test checks is the one the example describes. The test also checks a second requirement and a source node. This supports the distinct-paths obligation without weakening the main assertion.\n\nThe test carries a `{test}` reference to `requirements.sdk.graph.hierarchical-paths`. I found no gaps.",
